<title>AUSTRALIA: Australian s/adj March employment fell 33,900.</title>
<headline>Australian s/adj March employment fell 33,900.</headline>
<dateline>CANBERRA 1997-04-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Australia's seasonally adjusted estimate of the number of employed persons fell 33,900 in March to 8.38 million, against 8.42 million in February and 8.29 million a year earlier, the Bureau of Statistics said.</p>
<p>The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate was at 8.7 percent against 8.8 percent in February, compared with 8.5 percent in March 1996. Full-time employment fell 30,400.</p>
<p>Economists had on average expected employment to rise around 13,500 in March, seasonally-adjusted, with the unemployment rate dipping to 8.7 percent.  </p>
<p>AUSTRALIAN UNEMPLOYMENT</p>
<p>    (Seasonally adj)     March 97     Feb 97     March 96</p>
<p>    Unemployment Rate     8.7 pct     8.8 pct	8.5 pct</p>
<p>    Employed		 8.38 million    8.42 million     8.29 million</p>
<p>    Unemployed		803,000     810,800	770,800</p>
<p>    Participation Rate   63.3 pct    63.7 pct     63.5 pct</p>
<p>(Unadjusted)</p>
<p>    Unemployment Rate     9.2 pct     9.8 pct	8.9 pct</p>
<p>    Employed		 8.39 million    8.35 million     8.30 million</p>
<p>    Unemployed		845,400     906,100	810,900</p>
<p>    Participation Rate   63.7 pct    63.9 pct     63.8 pct</p>
<p>(Trend Estimates)</p>
<p>    Unemployment Rate    8.7 pct	8.7 pct	8.5 pct</p>
<p>    Employed		8.41 million     8.41 million     8.31 million</p>
<p>    Unemployed	     801,400	798,300	770,900</p>
<p>    Participation Rate  63.5 pct     63.5 pct     63.6 pct</p>
<p>The bureau said the 30,400 fall in full-time employment in March was mainly due a fall in female full-time employment of 30,100.</p>
<p>Overall, male employment rose 7,400, while female employment fell 41,300.</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
